window.DVA_EN["s15"] = [{"chapter":"CICD","q":"CI (Continuous Integration) là gì?","eq":"Continuous Integration (CI)","en":"Continuous Integration (CI)\n• Developers push the code to a code repository often (e.g., GitHub, CodeCommit,\nBitbucket…)\n• A testing / build server checks the code as soon as it’s pushed (CodeBuild, Jenkins CI, …)\n• The developer gets feedback about the tests and checks that have passed / failed\n• Find bugs early, then fix bugs\n• Deliver faster as the code is tested\n• Deploy often\n• Happier developers, as they’re unblocked"},{"chapter":"CICD","q":"CD (Continuous Delivery) là gì?","eq":"Continuous Delivery (CD)","en":"Continuous Delivery (CD)\n• Ensures that the software can be released reliably whenever needed\n• Ensures deployments happen often and are quick\n• Shift away from “one release every 3 months” to ”5 releases a day”\n• That usually means automated deployment (e.g., CodeDeploy, Jenkins\nCD, Spinnaker, …)\n---\nContinuous Integration (CI)\n• Developers push the code to a code repository often (e.g., GitHub, CodeCommit,\nBitbucket…)\n• A testing / build server checks the code as soon as it’s pushed (CodeBuild, Jenkins CI, …)\n• The developer gets feedback about the tests and checks that have passed / failed\n• Find bugs early, then fix bugs\n• Deliver faster as the code is tested\n• Deploy often\n• Happier developers, as they’re unblocked"},{"chapter":"CICD","q":"5 service chính trong AWS CICD?","eq":"CICD – Introduction","en":"CICD – Introduction\n• We have learned how to:\n• Create AWS resources, manually (fundamentals)\n• Interact with AWS programmatically (AWS CLI)\n• Deploy code to AWS using Elastic Beanstalk\n• All these manual steps make it very likely for us to do mistakes!\n• We would like our code “in a repository” and have it deployed onto AWS\n• Automatically\n• The right way\n• Making sure it’s tested before being deployed\n• With possibility to go into different stages (dev, test, staging, prod)\n• With manual approval where needed\n• To be a proper AWS developer… we need to learn AWS CICD\n---\nCICD – Introduction\n• This section is all about automating the deployment we’ve done so far while adding increased safety\n• We’ll learn about:\n• AWS CodeCommit – storing our code\n• AWS CodePipeline – automating our pipeline from code to Elastic Beanstalk\n• AWS CodeBuild – building and testing our code\n• AWS CodeDeploy – deploying the code to EC2 instances (not Elastic Beanstalk)\n• AWS CodeArtifact – store, publish, and share software packages\n• AWS CodeGuru – automated code reviews using Machine Learning"},{"chapter":"CodeCommit","q":"CodeCommit có ưu điểm gì?","eq":"AWS CodeCommit","en":"AWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools\n---\nCodeCommit – Security\n• Interactions are done using Git (standard)\n• Authentication\n• SSH Keys – AWS Users can configure SSH keys in their IAM Console\n• HTTPS – with AWS CLI Credential helper or Git Credentials for IAM user\n• Authorization\n• IAM policies to manage users/roles permissions to repositories\n• Encryption\n• Repositories are automatically encrypted at rest using AWS KMS\n• Encrypted in transit (can only use HTTPS or SSH – both secure)\n• Cross-account Access\n• Do NOT share your SSH keys or your AWS credentials\n• Use an IAM Role in your AWS account and use AWS STS (AssumeRole API)"},{"chapter":"CodeCommit","q":"CodeCommit Security: authentication, authorization, encryption?","eq":"CodeCommit – Security","en":"CodeCommit – Security\n• Interactions are done using Git (standard)\n• Authentication\n• SSH Keys – AWS Users can configure SSH keys in their IAM Console\n• HTTPS – with AWS CLI Credential helper or Git Credentials for IAM user\n• Authorization\n• IAM policies to manage users/roles permissions to repositories\n• Encryption\n• Repositories are automatically encrypted at rest using AWS KMS\n• Encrypted in transit (can only use HTTPS or SSH – both secure)\n• Cross-account Access\n• Do NOT share your SSH keys or your AWS credentials\n• Use an IAM Role in your AWS account and use AWS STS (AssumeRole API)"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline stages & providers?","eq":"AWS CodePipeline","en":"AWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline Artifacts?","eq":"CodePipeline – Artifacts","en":"CodePipeline – Artifacts\n• Each pipeline stage can create artifacts\n• Artifacts stored in an S3 bucket and passed on to the next stage\n---\nAWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"Troubleshoot CodePipeline thế nào?","eq":"CodePipeline –Troubleshooting","en":"CodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls\n---\nAWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild là gì?","eq":"AWS CodeBuild","en":"AWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging\n---\nCodeBuild – Supported Environments\n• Java\n• Ruby\n• Python\n• Go\n• Node.js\n• Android\n• .NET Core\n• PHP\n• Docker – extend any environment you like"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild: source, buildspec, logs?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup\n---\nAWS CodeBuild\n• Source – CodeCommit, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build instructions: Code file buildspec.yml or insert manually in Console\n• Output logs can be stored in Amazon S3 & CloudWatch Logs\n• Use CloudWatch Metrics to monitor build statistics\n• Use EventBridge to detect failed builds and trigger notifications\n• Use CloudWatch Alarms to notify if you need “thresholds” for failures\n• Build Projects can be defined within CodePipeline or CodeBuild"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"buildspec.yml có các section gì?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"Environment hỗ trợ của CodeBuild?","eq":"CodeBuild – Supported Environments","en":"CodeBuild – Supported Environments\n• Java\n• Ruby\n• Python\n• Go\n• Node.js\n• Android\n• .NET Core\n• PHP\n• Docker – extend any environment you like\n---\nCodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy là gì?","eq":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time\n---\nCodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy EC2/On-premises: platform & deployment speed?","eq":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time\n---\nCodeDeploy – ECS Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate the deployment of a new ECS Task\nDefinition\n• Only Blue/Green Deployments\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• ECSCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• ECSCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy Agent: yêu cầu gì?","eq":"CodeDeploy Agent","en":"CodeDeploy Agent\n• The CodeDeploy Agent must be running on the EC2 instances as a pre- requisites\n• It can be installed and updated automatically if you’re using Systems\nManager\n• The EC2 Instances must have sufficient permissions to access Amazon S3 to get deployment bundles\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy cho Lambda: các config?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy cho ECS?","eq":"CodeDeploy – ECS Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – ECS Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate the deployment of a new ECS Task\nDefinition\n• Only Blue/Green Deployments\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• ECSCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• ECSCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"Deploy to ASG: In-place vs Blue/Green?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Deploy to an ASG","en":"CodeDeploy – Deploy to an ASG\n• In-place Deployment\n• Updates existing EC2 instances\n• Newly created EC2 instances by an\nASG will also get automated\ndeployments\n• Blue/Green Deployment\n• A new Auto-Scaling Group is created (settings are copied)\n• Choose how long to keep the old\nEC2 instances (old ASG)\n• Must be using an ELB\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"Redeploy & Rollback?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Redeploy & Rollbacks","en":"CodeDeploy – Redeploy & Rollbacks\n• Rollback = redeploy a previously deployed revision of your application\n• Deployments can be rolled back:\n• Automatically – rollback when a deployment fails or rollback when a CloudWatch\nAlarm thresholds are met\n• Manually\n• Disable Rollbacks — do not perform rollbacks for this deployment\n• If a roll back happens, CodeDeploy redeploys the last known good revision as a new deployment (not a restored version)"},{"chapter":"CodeArtifact","q":"CodeArtifact là gì?","eq":"AWS CodeArtifact","en":"AWS CodeArtifact\n• Software packages depend on each other to be built (also called code dependencies), and new ones are created\n• Storing and retrieving these dependencies is called artifact management\n• Traditionally you need to setup your own artifact management system\n• CodeArtifact is a secure, scalable, and cost-effective artifact management for software development\n• Works with common dependency management tools such as Maven,\nGradle, npm, yarn, twine, pip, and NuGet\n• Developers and CodeBuild can then retrieve dependencies straight from\nCodeArtifact\n---\nCodeArtifact – Resource Policy\n• Can be used to authorize another account to access CodeArtifact\n• A given principal can either read all the packages in a repository or none of them"},{"chapter":"CodeGuru","q":"CodeGuru Reviewer vs Profiler?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler","en":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru Reviewer\n• Identify critical issues, security vulnerabilities, and hard-to-find bugs\n• Example: common coding best practices, resource leaks, security detection, input validation\n• Uses Machine Learning and automated reasoning\n• Hard-learned lessons across millions of code reviews on 1000s of open-source and Amazon repositories\n• Supports Java and Python\n• Integrates with GitHub, Bitbucket, and\nAWS CodeCommit"},{"chapter":"CodeGuru","q":"CodeGuru Profiler agent config?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration","en":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application"},{"chapter":"CodeCommit","q":"CodeCommit: branch, pull request & notification?","eq":"AWS CodeCommit","en":"AWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools\n---\nCodeCommit – Security\n• Interactions are done using Git (standard)\n• Authentication\n• SSH Keys – AWS Users can configure SSH keys in their IAM Console\n• HTTPS – with AWS CLI Credential helper or Git Credentials for IAM user\n• Authorization\n• IAM policies to manage users/roles permissions to repositories\n• Encryption\n• Repositories are automatically encrypted at rest using AWS KMS\n• Encrypted in transit (can only use HTTPS or SSH – both secure)\n• Cross-account Access\n• Do NOT share your SSH keys or your AWS credentials\n• Use an IAM Role in your AWS account and use AWS STS (AssumeRole API)"},{"chapter":"CodeCommit","q":"CodeCommit: migrate repo & giới hạn cần biết?","eq":"AWS CodeCommit","en":"AWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools\n---\nCodeCommit – Security\n• Interactions are done using Git (standard)\n• Authentication\n• SSH Keys – AWS Users can configure SSH keys in their IAM Console\n• HTTPS – with AWS CLI Credential helper or Git Credentials for IAM user\n• Authorization\n• IAM policies to manage users/roles permissions to repositories\n• Encryption\n• Repositories are automatically encrypted at rest using AWS KMS\n• Encrypted in transit (can only use HTTPS or SSH – both secure)\n• Cross-account Access\n• Do NOT share your SSH keys or your AWS credentials\n• Use an IAM Role in your AWS account and use AWS STS (AssumeRole API)"},{"chapter":"CodeCommit","q":"CodeCommit vs GitHub — khi nào chọn cái nào?","eq":"CodeCommit vs. GitHub","en":"CodeCommit vs. GitHub\n---\nAWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline cấu trúc: stage, action, action group?","eq":"AWS CodePipeline","en":"AWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"Manual approval action hoạt động thế nào?","eq":"AWS CodePipeline","en":"AWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline cross-region & cross-account?","eq":"AWS CodePipeline","en":"AWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline trigger: polling vs event?","eq":"AWS CodePipeline","en":"AWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CodePipeline","q":"CodePipeline: chi phí & artifact retention?","eq":"CodePipeline – Artifacts","en":"CodePipeline – Artifacts\n• Each pipeline stage can create artifacts\n• Artifacts stored in an S3 bucket and passed on to the next stage\n---\nAWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"buildspec.yml: các phase và thứ tự?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild: biến môi trường & secrets?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup\n---\nAWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild cache: S3 vs local?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup\n---\nAWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild trong VPC & private registry?","eq":"AWS CodeBuild","en":"AWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging\n---\nCodeBuild – Supported Environments\n• Java\n• Ruby\n• Python\n• Go\n• Node.js\n• Android\n• .NET Core\n• PHP\n• Docker – extend any environment you like"},{"chapter":"CodeBuild","q":"CodeBuild: báo cáo test & coverage?","eq":"AWS CodeBuild","en":"AWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging\n---\nCodeBuild – Supported Environments\n• Java\n• Ruby\n• Python\n• Go\n• Node.js\n• Android\n• .NET Core\n• PHP\n• Docker – extend any environment you like"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy deployment configuration cho EC2/ASG?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2","en":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2\n• Define how to deploy the application using appspec.yml + Deployment\nStrategy\n• Will do In -place update to your fleet of EC2 instances\n• Can use hooks to verify the deployment after each deployment phase\n---\nCodeDeploy – In -Place Deployment"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"Deployment configuration cho Lambda?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nCodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2\n• Define how to deploy the application using appspec.yml + Deployment\nStrategy\n• Will do In -place update to your fleet of EC2 instances\n• Can use hooks to verify the deployment after each deployment phase"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"AppSpec: cấu trúc cho EC2 vs Lambda vs ECS?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy lifecycle event hooks (EC2/On-prem)?","eq":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time\n---\nCodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy agent & deployment group?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2","en":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2\n• Define how to deploy the application using appspec.yml + Deployment\nStrategy\n• Will do In -place update to your fleet of EC2 instances\n• Can use hooks to verify the deployment after each deployment phase\n---\nCodeDeploy – In -Place Deployment"},{"chapter":"CodeDeploy","q":"CodeDeploy Blue/Green cho ECS?","eq":"CodeDeploy – ECS Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – ECS Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate the deployment of a new ECS Task\nDefinition\n• Only Blue/Green Deployments\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• ECSLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• ECSCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• ECSCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nCodeDeploy – EC2/On-premises Platform\n• Can deploy to EC2 Instances & on-premises servers\n• Perform in-place deployments or blue/green deployments\n• Must run the CodeDeploy Agent on the target instances\n• Define deployment speed\n• AllAtOnce: Deploys to all instances at the same time, most downtime but fastest\n• HalfAtATime: Deploys to half of the instances at a time, reduces capacity by 50%\n• OneAtATime: Deploys to one instance at a time, slowest but least impact capacity\n• Custom: define a custom percentage of instances to deploy at a time"},{"chapter":"CodeArtifact","q":"CodeArtifact: domain, repository, upstream?","eq":"AWS CodeArtifact","en":"AWS CodeArtifact\n• Software packages depend on each other to be built (also called code dependencies), and new ones are created\n• Storing and retrieving these dependencies is called artifact management\n• Traditionally you need to setup your own artifact management system\n• CodeArtifact is a secure, scalable, and cost-effective artifact management for software development\n• Works with common dependency management tools such as Maven,\nGradle, npm, yarn, twine, pip, and NuGet\n• Developers and CodeBuild can then retrieve dependencies straight from\nCodeArtifact\n---\nCodeArtifact – Resource Policy\n• Can be used to authorize another account to access CodeArtifact\n• A given principal can either read all the packages in a repository or none of them"},{"chapter":"CodeArtifact","q":"CodeArtifact auth: token & CodeBuild?","eq":"AWS CodeArtifact","en":"AWS CodeArtifact\n• Software packages depend on each other to be built (also called code dependencies), and new ones are created\n• Storing and retrieving these dependencies is called artifact management\n• Traditionally you need to setup your own artifact management system\n• CodeArtifact is a secure, scalable, and cost-effective artifact management for software development\n• Works with common dependency management tools such as Maven,\nGradle, npm, yarn, twine, pip, and NuGet\n• Developers and CodeBuild can then retrieve dependencies straight from\nCodeArtifact\n---\nCodeArtifact – Resource Policy\n• Can be used to authorize another account to access CodeArtifact\n• A given principal can either read all the packages in a repository or none of them"},{"chapter":"CodeArtifact","q":"CodeArtifact vs S3 artifact bucket?","eq":"AWS CodeArtifact","en":"AWS CodeArtifact\n• Software packages depend on each other to be built (also called code dependencies), and new ones are created\n• Storing and retrieving these dependencies is called artifact management\n• Traditionally you need to setup your own artifact management system\n• CodeArtifact is a secure, scalable, and cost-effective artifact management for software development\n• Works with common dependency management tools such as Maven,\nGradle, npm, yarn, twine, pip, and NuGet\n• Developers and CodeBuild can then retrieve dependencies straight from\nCodeArtifact\n---\nCodeArtifact – Resource Policy\n• Can be used to authorize another account to access CodeArtifact\n• A given principal can either read all the packages in a repository or none of them"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AWS AppConfig là gì và giải quyết vấn đề gì?","eq":"AWS CodeCommit","en":"AWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools\n---\nAWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig: application, environment, configuration profile?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler","en":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig deployment strategy là gì?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2","en":"CodeDeploy – Deployment to EC2\n• Define how to deploy the application using appspec.yml + Deployment\nStrategy\n• Will do In -place update to your fleet of EC2 instances\n• Can use hooks to verify the deployment after each deployment phase\n---\nCodeDeploy – In -Place Deployment"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig rollback hoạt động thế nào?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Redeploy & Rollbacks","en":"CodeDeploy – Redeploy & Rollbacks\n• Rollback = redeploy a previously deployed revision of your application\n• Deployments can be rolled back:\n• Automatically – rollback when a deployment fails or rollback when a CloudWatch\nAlarm thresholds are met\n• Manually\n• Disable Rollbacks — do not perform rollbacks for this deployment\n• If a roll back happens, CodeDeploy redeploys the last known good revision as a new deployment (not a restored version)"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig: validators (kiểm tra config)?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration","en":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"Feature flag trong AppConfig là gì?","eq":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform","en":"CodeDeploy – Lambda Platform\n• CodeDeploy can help you automate traffic shift for Lambda aliases\n• Feature is integrated within the SAM framework\n• Linear: grow traffic every N minutes until 100%\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery3Minutes\n• LambdaLinear10PercentEvery10Minutes\n• Canary: try X percent then 100%\n• LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes\n• LambdaCanary10Percent30Minutes\n• AllAtOnce: immediate\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"App đọc config từ AppConfig bằng cách nào?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration","en":"Amazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig khác Parameter Store / Secrets Manager thế nào?","eq":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml","en":"CodeBuild – buildspec.yml\n• buildspec.yml file must be at the root of your code\n• env – define environment variables\n• variables – plaintext variables\n• parameter-store – variables stored in SSM Parameter Store\n• secrets-manager – variables stored in AWS Secrets Manager\n• phases – specify commands to run:\n• install – install dependencies you may need for your build\n• pre_build – final commands to execute before build\n• Build – actual build commands\n• post_build – finishing touches (e.g., zip output)\n• artifacts – what to upload to S3 (encrypted with KMS)\n• cache – files to cache (usually dependencies) to S3 for future build speedup"},{"chapter":"AppConfig","q":"AppConfig tích hợp với service nào?","eq":"AWS CodeBuild","en":"AWS CodeBuild\n• A fully managed continuous integration (CI) service\n• Continuous scaling (no servers to manage or provision – no build queue)\n• Compile source code, run tests, produce software packages, …\n• Alternative to other build tools (e.g., Jenkins)\n• Charged per minute for compute resources (time it takes to complete the builds)\n• Leverages Docker under the hood for reproducible builds\n• Use prepackaged Docker images or create your own custom Docker image\n• Security:\n• Integration with KMS for encryption of build artifacts\n• IAM for CodeBuild permissions, and VPC for network security\n• AWS CloudTrail for API calls logging\n---\nCodePipeline –Troubleshooting\n• For CodePipeline Pipeline/Action/Stage Execution State Changes\n• Use CloudWatch Events (Amazon EventBridge). Example:\n• You can create events for failed pipelines\n• You can create events for cancelled stages\n• If CodePipeline fails a stage, your pipeline stops, and you can get information in the console\n• If pipeline can’t perform an action, make sure the “IAM Service Role” attached does have enough IAM permissions (IAM Policy)\n• AWS CloudTrail can be used to audit AWS API calls"},{"chapter":"CloudShell & Amazon Q","q":"AWS CloudShell là gì?","eq":"AWS CodeCommit","en":"AWS CodeCommit\n• Git repositories can be expensive\n• The industry includes GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, …\n• And AWS CodeCommit:\n• Private Git repositories\n• No size limit on repositories (scale seamlessly)\n• Fully managed, highly available\n• Code only in AWS Cloud account => increased security and compliance\n• Security (encrypted, access control, …)\n• Integrated with Jenkins, AWS CodeBuild, and other CI tools\n---\nAWS CodePipeline\n• Visual Workflow to orchestrate your CICD\n• Source – CodeCommit, ECR, S3, Bitbucket, GitHub\n• Build – CodeBuild, Jenkins, CloudBees, TeamCity\n• Test – CodeBuild, AWS Device Farm, 3rd party tools, …\n• Deploy – CodeDeploy, Elastic Beanstalk, CloudFormation, ECS, S3, …\n• Invoke – Lambda, Step Functions\n• Consists of stages:\n• Each stage can have sequential actions and/or parallel actions\n• Example: Build Test Deploy Load Testing …\n• Manual approval can be defined at any stage"},{"chapter":"CloudShell & Amazon Q","q":"CloudShell: giới hạn và lưu trữ?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler","en":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"},{"chapter":"CloudShell & Amazon Q","q":"Amazon Q Developer là gì?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler","en":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"},{"chapter":"CloudShell & Amazon Q","q":"Amazon Q Developer khác CodeGuru thế nào?","eq":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler","en":"Amazon CodeGuru Profiler\n• Helps understand the runtime behavior of your application\n• Example: identify if your application is consuming excessive CPU capacity on a logging routine\n• Features:\n• Identify and remove code inefficiencies\n• Improve application performance (e.g., reduce CPU utilization)\n• Decrease compute costs\n• Provides heap summary (identify which objects using up memory)\n• Anomaly Detection\n• Support applications running on AWS or on- premise\n• Minimal overhead on application\n---\nAmazon CodeGuru – Agent Configuration\n• MaxStackDepth – the maximum depth of the stacks in the code that is represented in the profile\n• Example: if CodeGuru Profiler finds a method A, which calls method B, which calls method C, which calls method D, then the depth is 4\n• If the MaxStackDepth is set to 2, then the profiler evaluates A and B\n• MemoryUsageLimitPercent – the memory percentage used by the profiler\n• MinimumTimeForReportingInMilliseconds – the minimum time between sending reports (milliseconds)\n• ReportingIntervalInMilliseconds – the reporting interval used to report profiles (milliseconds)\n• SamplingIntervalInMilliseconds – the sampling interval that is used to profile samples (milliseconds)\n• Reduce to have a higher sampling rate"}];
